{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Educational Technology Evaluation","title":"Module 5 Evaluation Studio","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting module 5 evaluation studio to equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"08","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes shows a defensible decision in CAT 531"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/05/07"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_annotation_signal"],"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-03":["cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Module 5 Evaluation Studio\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Module 5 Evaluation Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-08-module-5-evaluation-studio.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Module 5 Evaluation Studio: Module 5 Evaluation Studio, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe studio is where the seven moves of this module stop being separate skills and become one brief. Writing the question before the rating, separating evidence from vendor claims, splitting usability from accessibility from equity, costing the tool honestly, gating the fit matrix, conditioning the recommendation, and surviving peer review are not a checklist to staple together. They have to converge on a single decision, and the hard part is that they often point in different directions. A brief that stacks the seven sections side by side is not a brief; it is a binder. This section teaches synthesis: resolving the tensions between sections into one coherent, defensible recommendation.\n\nThe capstone matters because a real adoption decision is exactly this composite. The cost section may favor one tool while the equity section forbids it, and the brief's job is not to hide that conflict behind a tidy total but to name it and resolve it in the open. A reader should be able to see the trade-off you made and the evidence that would reopen it.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Assemble the seven module sections into a single brief whose recommendation traces back to the opening question.\n- Surface a tension between sections (for example cost versus equity) instead of letting one section quietly win.\n- Resolve the tension explicitly, stating which value gates which, and why.\n- State the evidence that would reopen the decision, so the brief is revisable rather than final.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nIn the studio you assemble a full brief for a video-annotation tool, pulling together the question, the evidence-versus-vendor-claims read, the usability and accessibility checks, the cost ledger, the fit matrix, the recommendation, and the peer-review pass. Each section reads fine on its own. But the cost section favored Tool A on price, while the equity section flagged that Tool A requires a personal device, excluding 5 students who rely on shared school devices, and the draft recommendation simply says \"adopt Tool A,\" having quietly followed cost.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not which tool the brief names. It is whether the brief surfaces the cost-versus-equity conflict it contains and resolves it on purpose, rather than letting the cheapest option drift to the top unexamined.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nSynthesis is the act of making the sections argue with each other and then settling the argument in writing. Three skills define it:\n\n- **Trace the thread.** Every section should connect back to the opening evaluation question. If the recommendation answers a different question than the one the brief opened with, the thread is broken and the synthesis has failed.\n- **Surface the tension.** When two sections point opposite ways, the worst outcome is silence: the brief follows one and never mentions the other. Name the conflict in a sentence: \"cost favors Tool A; equity forbids it.\"\n- **Resolve with a gate.** Decide which value governs and say why. A participation barrier that excludes 5 students is not a cost the class can trade away, so the equity finding gates the cost preference. The resolution is a judgment, and the brief defends it rather than burying it.\n\nA coherent brief also states its **reopening condition**: the future fact that would change the decision. \"If the school provisions one-to-one devices, Tool A becomes viable and we re-evaluate.\" This keeps the recommendation honest as a current best judgment rather than a permanent verdict, and it tells a reader exactly what to watch for.\n\nThe three course anchors operate at the level of the whole brief here. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** checks that the assembled recommendation still serves the learning the question named, not a feature that crept in along the way. Selwyn's **critical edtech studies** are usually the source of the tension that synthesis must resolve, since equity and access conflicts rarely sit quietly. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** can introduce a late gate, a data-handling fact from one section that overrides preferences built up across the others.\n\nSynthesis follows an order. Put the moves in the sequence that turns a binder of seven sections into one coherent brief.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Trace the thread: confirm every section connects back to the opening evaluation question\",\"Surface the tension: name in one sentence where two sections point opposite ways (cost favors A; equity forbids it)\",\"Resolve with a gate: decide which value governs and defend it (a participation barrier excluding 5 students gates the cost preference)\",\"State the reopening condition: name the future fact that would change the decision (one-to-one devices make Tool A viable again)\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your assembled video-annotation brief reads as seven correct sections with no conflict and a tidy 'adopt Tool A' that matches the cost section. What does that smoothness most likely indicate?\" explanation=\"A brief with no visible tension has usually hidden one. Here the cost section favored Tool A while the equity section flagged that Tool A excludes 5 shared-device students; the draft quietly followed the cleanest number and left the conflict unspoken. Real composite decisions contain tensions; synthesis means naming and resolving them, not averaging them away.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The tool is unusually clean, so no synthesis is needed.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A tension was probably hidden; the cost-versus-equity conflict was never surfaced or resolved.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The brief is finished because every section is individually correct.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft often hides the real decision behind a vague improvement claim:\n\n> I made the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nNotice the shift: the artifact is no longer just polished. The revision names the evidence source and preserves the reason a suggestion was accepted or rejected.\n\n### Worked Example: Assembling One Coherent Brief\n\nScenario: in the studio you assemble a full brief for a video-annotation tool, pulling the seven earlier sections together.\n\n1. **Stacked the parts (before).** The draft lists seven sections side by side: question, evidence vs vendor claims, usability and accessibility, cost, fit matrix, recommendation, peer review. Each reads fine alone, but the recommendation simply says \"Adopt,\" with no thread back to the question.\n2. **Found the internal tension.** The cost section favored Tool A on price. The equity section flagged that Tool A requires a personal device, excluding 5 students who rely on shared school devices. The two sections pointed in opposite directions, and the draft had quietly followed cost.\n3. **Synthesized the tension instead of hiding it.** The revision states the conflict in one line, then resolves it: device-dependent exclusion is a participation barrier the class cannot accept, so the equity finding gates the cost preference.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The closing reads: \"Recommendation: adopt Tool B (slightly higher cost) for one term. This answers our original question (equitable annotation access) and resolves the cost-vs-equity tension in favor of access. What would change this: if the school provisions 1:1 devices, Tool A becomes viable and we would re-evaluate. We rejected hiding the cost-equity conflict because a reviewer must see the trade-off we made.\"\n\nThe capstone is defensible because the parts now converge on one decision, the trade-off is explicit, and the brief states what evidence would reopen it.\n\nNow resolve the video-annotation brief yourself. The scenario tests whether you surface the cost-versus-equity tension or let the cheapest option drift to the top.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Assembling the video-annotation brief: cost versus equity\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Your seven sections each read fine. The cost section favors Tool A on price; the equity section flags that Tool A requires a personal device, excluding 5 students who rely on shared school devices. The draft recommendation says \\\"adopt Tool A.\\\" What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep \\\"adopt Tool A\\\"; it has the cleanest cost number\",\"next\":\"cost\",\"feedback\":\"You let one section win by default and left the conflict unspoken.\"},{\"label\":\"Read the recommendation first and ask which section it followed\",\"next\":\"trace\",\"feedback\":\"You check the thread before closing the brief.\"}]},\"cost\":{\"prompt\":\"You ship \\\"adopt Tool A.\\\" In the first week, 5 shared-device students cannot annotate the assessed video at all. What did the brief do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Followed the cleanest number and hid the cost-versus-equity conflict\",\"next\":\"cost_end\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Assembly is not synthesis; the tension was never named.\"}]},\"cost_end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: stacking seven sound sections does not make a coherent brief. Cost carried a clean number and equity carried a harder-to-quantify burden, so cost won by default and 5 students were excluded from the assessed task.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the trap: a tension averaged into silence.\",\"choices\":[]},\"trace\":{\"prompt\":\"You trace the recommendation and find it matches only the cost section; the equity finding (5 students excluded) was never reconciled. What is the next move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Average cost and equity into a single score and pick the higher one\",\"next\":\"average\",\"feedback\":\"Averaging buries the conflict instead of resolving it.\"},{\"label\":\"State the conflict in one line, then decide which value gates which\",\"next\":\"resolve\",\"feedback\":\"You make the conflict speak before settling it.\"}]},\"average\":{\"prompt\":\"A blended score still lets Tool A\\\\u2019s price offset the exclusion of 5 students from the core task. Is device-dependent exclusion a cost the class can trade away?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No; a participation barrier locking 5 students out of the assessed task cannot be bought off\",\"next\":\"resolve\",\"feedback\":\"Right. This is a gate, not a tradeable score.\"}]},\"resolve\":{\"prompt\":\"You write: \\\"Cost favors Tool A; equity forbids it because device dependence excludes 5 students from the core annotation activity.\\\" Which value gates which?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The equity finding gates the cost preference; recommend Tool B at slightly higher cost\",\"next\":\"reopen\",\"feedback\":\"You resolve the tension on purpose and defend the choice.\"},{\"label\":\"The cost preference gates equity; keep Tool A\",\"next\":\"average\",\"feedback\":\"A price cannot offset locking 5 students out of the assessed task; reconsider.\"}]},\"reopen\":{\"prompt\":\"Final move: keep the recommendation honest as a current judgment. What do you add?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"A reopening condition: if the school provisions one-to-one devices, Tool A becomes viable and we re-evaluate\",\"next\":\"end\",\"feedback\":\"You make the decision revisable rather than permanent.\"}]},\"end\":{\"prompt\":\"Lesson: adopt Tool B for one term. It answers the opening question (equitable annotation access) and resolves the cost-versus-equity tension in favor of access, with a stated reopening condition (one-to-one devices). You rejected hiding the conflict because a reviewer must see the trade-off the decision turned on.\",\"feedback\":\"This is synthesis: one coherent decision, the trade-off named, the reopening fact stated.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n**Recent Evidence.** The late gate is concrete, not hypothetical. If Tool A's required personal account shares children's behavioral data with third parties for targeted advertising, the FTC's amended COPPA Rule (effective June 23, 2025, with full compliance required by April 22, 2026) requires separate verifiable parental consent for that disclosure and bars retaining children's data longer than reasonably necessary; and FERPA's school-official exception (20 U.S.C. 1232g) permits a vendor to receive education records without parental consent only under the district's direct contractual control, with onward disclosure barred. In the worked example, the gates stack: device dependence excludes 5 students AND, if Tool A's account creation shares behavioral data with third parties, a COPPA/FERPA data-handling gate stacks on top of the equity gate.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the opening question as the thread, the cross-section tensions as the real work, and the final recommendation as the place those tensions are resolved in the open. A capstone brief is defensible only when its recommendation traces to its question and its trade-offs are named rather than hidden.\n\nBefore you submit the assembled brief, run three checks across the whole thing:\n\n1. **Thread check:** Does the recommendation answer the question the brief opened with, in the same terms?\n2. **Tension check:** Where do two sections point opposite ways, and has the brief named that conflict instead of silently following one?\n3. **Reopen check:** What future fact would change the decision, and is it stated?\n\nIf the brief reads as seven correct sections with no conflict and no resolution, it has probably hidden a tension rather than resolved one, and the synthesis is incomplete.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating assembly as synthesis: pasting seven sound sections together does not produce a coherent brief if they never confront each other. The second is letting one section win by default, usually cost, because it carries a clean number while equity carries a harder-to-quantify burden. The third is presenting the recommendation as final, with no statement of what would reopen it, which makes a current judgment masquerade as a permanent truth.\n\nA defensible capstone names its internal tension, resolves it with a stated gate, threads the recommendation back to the opening question, and declares its reopening condition.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nLay the seven sections of the video-annotation brief in front of you and resist closing it yet. Read the recommendation first and ask which section it followed. It says \"adopt Tool A,\" and tracing back, that matches the cost section and nothing else; the equity section, which flagged that Tool A excludes 5 shared-device students, was never reconciled. The brief followed the cleanest number and left the conflict unspoken.\n\nMake the conflict speak. Write it in one line: cost favors Tool A, equity forbids it because device dependence excludes 5 students from the core annotation activity. Now decide which value governs and defend the choice. A participation barrier that locks 5 students out of the assessed task is not a price the class can pay, so the equity finding gates the cost preference, and the recommendation flips to Tool B at a slightly higher cost.\n\nClose by threading back and forward. Confirm that adopting Tool B answers the opening question, equitable annotation access, in the same words the brief began with. Then state the reopening condition: if the school provisions one-to-one devices, the device barrier disappears and Tool A returns to contention. A reviewer can now see one coherent decision, the trade-off behind it, and the exact fact that would revise it.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read an assembled brief, keep a map rather than a list: draw an arrow from the opening question to the recommendation and mark every place two sections pull in opposite directions. The quality of the synthesis lives at those marks. A brief with no marked tensions has either an unusually clean tool or, far more often, a conflict it declined to name.\n\nA strong reader of a capstone brief can answer four questions:\n\n1. Does the recommendation trace cleanly back to the opening question?\n2. Where do the sections conflict, and is each conflict named in the brief?\n3. Which value gates which in the resolution, and is the choice defended?\n4. What future fact would reopen the decision, and is it stated?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the brief is strong when the recommendation threads to the opening question, every cross-section tension is named and resolved with a stated gate, and a reopening condition is declared, so a mentor can audit not just each section but the seams between them. Treat a brief with no visible tension as a brief that hid one.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the brief so the cost-versus-equity tension is stated and resolved in favor of access, with Tool B recommended for one term and a reopening condition (one-to-one devices) named. I kept out the suggestion to present the sections as seven independent verdicts because it would have hidden the trade-off the decision actually turned on.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the synthesis stance beyond this module to any composite professional decision: a curriculum adoption, a grading-policy change, a schedule redesign. Each is built from sub-analyses that can conflict, and each is well made only when the conflicts are surfaced and resolved rather than averaged or ignored. The video-annotation brief is a rehearsal for the larger decisions where the cost-versus-equity pattern recurs under different names. Use the transfer to confirm that you can assemble many partial findings into one defensible recommendation with its tensions named, in any setting where the parts disagree.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-08-module-5-evaluation-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the equity-oriented edtech evaluation matrix with context-specific risk notes for CAT 531 05.08. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why assembling seven correct sections side by side is not the same as synthesizing them, and why a capstone brief with no visible tension is suspect rather than reassuring.\" answer=\"Assembly places seven sound sections next to each other, but a real adoption decision is a composite whose parts can point in opposite directions: cost can favor a tool that equity forbids. Synthesis makes those sections confront each other, names the conflict in the open, and resolves it by deciding which value gates which and why, then threads the result back to the opening question and states what would reopen it. A brief with no visible tension is suspect because genuine composite decisions almost always contain a conflict; a smooth, conflict-free brief has usually let one section (often cost, with its clean number) win by default and hidden the trade-off the decision actually turned on.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm the assembled brief threads its recommendation back to the opening question, names each cross-section tension, resolves it with a stated gate and a defended choice, and declares the future fact that would reopen the decision.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning et al. (2024), Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Selwyn, critical edtech studies (Distrusting Educational Technology, Routledge). https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- Selwyn, N. (2014), Data entry: Towards the critical study of digital data and education. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2014.921628\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Mouta et al. (2023), Uncovering blind spots in education ethics: Insights from a systematic literature review on artificial intelligence in education. https://doi.org/10.1007/s40593-023-00384-9\n- Federal Trade Commission (2025), Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule: Final amendments. https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/04/22/2025-05904/childrens-online-privacy-protection-rule\n- U.S. Department of Education, Student Privacy Policy Office, Responsibilities of third-party service providers under FERPA. https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/resources/responsibilities-third-party-service-providers-under-ferpa\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio","stem":"The Module 5 studio asks you to assemble a full evaluation brief. What makes the assembled brief coherent rather than a stack of separate parts?","options":["Each part is long and detailed.","The evaluation question, evidence, equity check, cost, fit matrix, and recommendation all point to the same decision and support one another.","Every section uses the same template heading.","The brief includes the maximum number of tools considered."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A capstone brief is coherent when its parts are linked: the recommendation answers the original question, and the evidence, equity, cost, and fit sections each justify that answer. Disconnected sections do not make a decision.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_sections_as_independent","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio","stem":"In assembling your brief, the cost section favors Tool A but the equity section flags that Tool A excludes students without devices. What does a strong synthesis do with this tension?","options":["Hide the equity finding so the brief reads cleanly.","Surface the tension explicitly and explain which consideration was prioritized and why, so the trade-off is visible.","Average the two sections into one score.","Drop whichever section is harder to defend."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Synthesis is not erasing conflict. A defensible capstone names the tension between sections and shows the reasoned choice, so a reader can see and contest the trade-off.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_synthesis_hides_tension","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation","cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You ask an AI tool to assemble your whole Module 5 brief from your notes. It returns a complete, fluent document. What is the responsible move?","options":["Submit it; the studio asked for a complete brief.","Use it as a draft, then verify that every claim traces to your own evidence and that the recommendation is one you can defend.","Ask the AI to also grade the brief.","Trust the AI assembly because it is internally consistent."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A fluent AI assembly can introduce claims you never verified and a recommendation you cannot defend. The studio assesses your judgment, so each part must be grounded in your own evidence before you own the brief.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_ai_assembles_brief","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat531_supplement_module_5_evaluation_studio"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-05-08-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_5_8_module_5_evaluation_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A reviewer notes that your finished brief is polished and complete but never states what evidence would change the recommendation. Why does this weaken the capstone?","options":["A complete brief never needs a reversal condition.","A defensible 